docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:10:This revision (2026-08-09, amended through 2026-08-12) folds in: the two pending amendments from the 2026-07-30 bindings walk (the refused `--no-wait` workspace retains a JSON refusal record, `status` returns it once then sweeps; the branch-protection audit's three named outcomes); the `Gatekeeper-agent` trailer (B6, boss-ruled 2026-07-31); and the credential rulings of 2026-08-09 (C1–C8 — the dedicated identity admitted early, the Unix-user boundary, scoped agent tokens, break-glass, the cooperative hook tier; their delta document, `docs/issues/queue/3-gatekeeper-credential-and-hook-bindings.md`, was retired 2026-08-10 when this specification became the rulings' sole normative home — provenance in git history and `md-review-records/`). The 2026-07-24 boss-walked core — request/reply, digest, trailers, concurrency, states, error catalog — is unchanged by all of this. Supersedes remain as before: the promotion-relay design (an earlier two-stage shape where changes reached main through a relay branch — replaced by this single gate), the entry-manifest append-a-row rule, the retired "land"/"landing" vocabulary (check-in vocabulary replaces it). The B-codes cited throughout (B1, B3c, B4a–B4d, B6) are the 2026-07-30 bindings-walk rulings, recorded in `docs/issues/queue/3-gatekeeper-build-bindings.md`. Throughout this document, "the boss" and "the user" are the same person: the human owner of the project.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:45:                                        under every user — C7)
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:146:- **The layout amendment (ruled 2026-08-09, C1/C3 — applying it needs an org owner):** the pusher role moves to a **dedicated GitHub account** — a collaborator with write on this one repository, not admin, never an org owner — and protection's push restriction names it alone (the account's name is recorded here verbatim when the amendment is applied). Chosen over the App/CI form this section previously sketched: an App or CI job relocates the gate into CI, which this design scopes out; a plain account keeps the gate a local program. Blast radius of a stolen token: commits to this one repository, nothing else. Owner power stays with the user; "no agent ever holds it" is the C3 target state, true at amendment application — until then `NedLern` is both pusher and owner, the live-state exception recorded above.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:147:- **The Unix-user boundary (C2) — where the enforcement actually lives:** GitHub restricts accounts, not processes, and on one machine every process of one Unix user reads the same credential files. So the main-capable credential is owned by a **dedicated Unix user**, unreadable by agent sessions, and agents invoke the program through a sudoers rule scoped to exactly it. That is the step at which "agents never push" becomes impossible rather than instructed; it also makes the gatekeeper literally an invocable service any agent on the box can call. The sudoers rule names a **root-owned copy of the program outside every checkout** (ruled 2026-08-10) — a rule pointing into a checkout would privilege whatever bytes an agent last wrote there. The copy keeps itself current from main automatically (no manual deploy step; a checked-in fix is live on the next call), which is safe because the gatekeeper's source joins the instruction-file class: changes reach main only with walked-approval evidence, enforced by the review-evidence check (slice 6). Activating the privileged lane therefore waits on slice 6. Working path: `/usr/local/lib/nedschorus-gatekeeper/git-gatekeeper.py`. Staleness of the deployed copy is fail-safe — a stale copy enforces the old contract and never runs bytes an agent planted in place directly — only walked-approved content from main; ownership, not freshness, carries the security guarantee, so a lag in self-update costs availability, never safety. Standing principle, project-wide: a deployed copy of anything keeps itself current from its source automatically rather than relying on a remembered deploy step.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:148:- **Agent credentials are scoped (C4):** each agent host holds a fine-grained token for this repository only — contents read/write (branch pushes are open; the push-less ruling covers main alone), issues write — never a classic all-repository token, and never the `workflow` scope (capability-by-landing class, nedschorus#31). Issue work is **scoped, not gated**: no invariant like "one writer to main" exists there, so discipline lives at the skill and hook rung, not at a gate.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:151:- **The test seams need no privileged-mode guard (C7 struck to zero, user-ruled 2026-08-10):** `--repo` and `--remote` exist so tests can hand the program throwaway repositories, and they behave identically under every user. The formerly planned refusals guarded nothing — a foreign GitHub remote dies at authentication because the credential is scoped to this one repository (C1), a non-GitHub remote (a local path) needs no credential and was never the protected asset, and refusing `--repo` would only move caller control to the working directory, not remove it. A hard-coded remote pin was considered as defense-in-depth against future credential misconfiguration and declined: the token's scope, set once by the user, is the guard.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:153:- **CLAUDE.md is documentation, never enforcement (boss ruling; his rationale verbatim: a python script does not read it, and different machines may carry different copies).** The same is true of harness hooks: they configure a harness, and only cooperating harnesses read them — which is why C6 is a convenience tier and C2 is the boundary. No *guarantee* in this design depends on either (the convenience and token savings do). The raw-push residual is handled at the configuration level, not prevented per-commit: a standing **branch-protection audit** at each session recycle (riding the fast-handoff supervisor's cycle, which runs several times a day; ruled 2026-08-12, replacing the retired "handoff scrub" anchor) checks the live protection settings against this design, with **three named outcomes** — `protection-ok` / `protection-wrong` (differing settings named) / `audit-failed` (gh missing, unauthenticated, API error) — failing loudly as its own outcome, never a silent skip into green (B3c). (It also covers the sibling residual while it exists: an agent-held owner credential could deliberately edit protection — same catch; C3 removes that class by taking owner power out of agent hands entirely.) A second detector — a scan of main's history for trailer-less commits, filing `draft` issues — was in this design until 2026-08-10 and is deleted (ruled at the subtraction review): once C2/C3 apply, a trailer-less commit can arise only from break-glass (user-approved by password in the moment), a protection failure (this audit catches the hole at the configuration level, though not the commits that used it), or a gatekeeper defect. Before C2 applies, an authorized raw push leaves configuration green and goes undetected — an accepted gap, by the same no-consumer reasoning that deleted the scan, closed when C2 lands. A detector whose findings have no consuming machinery is logging cost without value.
