./md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/codex-restate-good.md.stderr.log:164:- **The Unix-user boundary (C2) — where the enforcement actually lives:** GitHub restricts accounts, not processes, and on one machine every process of one Unix user reads the same credential files. So the main-capable credential is owned by a **dedicated Unix user**, unreadable by agent sessions, and agents invoke the program through a sudoers rule scoped to exactly it. That is the step at which "agents never push" becomes impossible rather than instructed; it also makes the gatekeeper literally an invocable service any agent on the box can call. The sudoers rule names a **root-owned copy of the program outside every checkout** (ruled 2026-08-10) — a rule pointing into a checkout would privilege whatever bytes an agent last wrote there. The copy keeps itself current from main automatically (no manual deploy step; a checked-in fix is live on the next call), which is safe because the gatekeeper's source joins the instruction-file class: changes reach main only with walked-approval evidence, enforced by the review-evidence check (slice 6). Activating the privileged lane therefore waits on slice 6. Working path: `/usr/local/lib/nedschorus-gatekeeper/git-gatekeeper.py`. Staleness of the deployed copy is fail-safe — a stale copy enforces the old contract and can never be made to run agent-written bytes; ownership, not freshness, carries the security guarantee, so a lag in self-update costs availability, never safety. Standing principle, project-wide: a deployed copy of anything keeps itself current from its source automatically rather than relying on a remembered deploy step.
