./md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/claude-restate-good.md:178:13. **"Agent credentials are scoped (C4): each agent host holds a fine-grained token for this repository only — contents read/write (branch pushes are open; the push-less ruling covers main alone), issues write — never a classic all-repository token, and never the `workflow` scope (capability-by-landing class, nedschorus#31)."** — Decision C4: every machine running agents holds a GitHub fine-grained personal access token limited to this one repository, granting exactly two permissions — read and write on repository contents, and write on issues. The parenthetical clarifies that "contents write" does allow pushing to branches other than `main`; the prohibition on agents pushing applies only to `main`. Explicitly excluded: GitHub's older "classic" token type, which grants access across all repositories the account can reach; and the `workflow` permission, which allows modifying CI workflow definitions. That exclusion is attributed to a category of capability described on issue 31 of the nedschorus repository, whose name I read as covering capabilities an agent could grant itself simply by getting a change onto `main`.
./md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/claude-restate-good.md:238:1. **"Which artifact classes, if any, are gated on review evidence from day one — the first class is now designated (boss-ruled 2026-08-04): instruction-bearing text (CLAUDE.md files, skills with their prompt templates, injected system prompts, the wiki), whose check-ins require walked-approval evidence; class definition, procedure, and guards on nedschorus#31."** — The open question is which categories of artifact, if any, must carry proof of review from the outset. Part of that question has since been answered: on 2026-08-04 the owner designated the first such category as text that instructs agents — meaning `CLAUDE.md` files, skill definitions together with the prompt templates they contain, system prompts inserted into agent contexts, and the project wiki. Check-ins of such material will be required to include evidence that the material was approved through the project's item-by-item review procedure. The precise definition of what falls in this category, the procedure to follow, and the safeguards around it are documented on issue 31 of the nedschorus repository.
